# PNETLab — privilege policy for the web user.
#
# Install as /etc/sudoers.d/pnetlab with mode 0440, owned root:root, and remove
# the upstream /etc/sudoers.d/unetlab. Validate with `visudo -cf` before
# deploying; a malformed file locks sudo out entirely.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this replaces
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Upstream ships, across /etc/sudoers and /etc/sudoers.d/unetlab:
#
#     www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/unetlab/wrappers/unl_wrapper
#     www-data ALL=(ALL)  NOPASSWD: ALL      <-- negates the line above
#     %www-data ALL=(ALL:ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
#     %unl     ALL=(ALL)  NOPASSWD: ALL
#
# sudo takes the last match, so `sudo -l -U www-data` resolves to
# "(ALL : ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL". The correctly-scoped first line never applied.
#
# The %unl grant is the worse of the two. Node start runs
#     useradd -c "Unified Networking Lab TID=N" -g unl -M -s /bin/bash unlN
# so every node session manufactures a Unix account with a login shell in a group
# holding unconditional root. Nothing runs under those accounts via sudo — they
# exist to own tap interfaces — so the grant has no legitimate use and is simply
# dropped. The accounts themselves were also never removed, by anything; that is
# now `unl_wrapper -a reap-tenant`, and the useradd grant went with it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this policy is, and is not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It is an allowlist of the 24 binaries the web layer actually invokes, derived
# from the tree and kept honest by tests/Security/SudoersPolicyTest.php, which
# fails if the code and this file drift apart.
#
# It is NOT a privilege boundary, and it should not be described as one.
# At least a dozen entries below are root-equivalent no matter how they are
# scoped — php and perl execute arbitrary code; tee, cp, mv, rm, chmod and chown
# write or take ownership of arbitrary paths; docker mounts the host filesystem;
# nohup and service launch arbitrary processes. Anyone who can influence the
# arguments to those can still become root.
#
# What it does buy, today:
#   - removes the %unl group grant entirely
#   - removes everything not on this list, so sudo su, sudo bash, sudo visudo,
#     sudo passwd and the rest stop working
#   - makes the remaining escalation surface explicit and countable rather than
#     unbounded, which is what lets it be reduced
#
# The end state is that every entry below except unl_wrapper is removed, with its
# behaviour moved behind the wrapper. Entries marked ROOT-EQUIVALENT should go
# first. Until then, treat a compromise of the web layer as a host compromise.

Defaults:www-data    !requiretty, umask_override, umask=0002

# --- The platform wrapper: the intended entry point ------------------------
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/unetlab/wrappers/unl_wrapper
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/unetlab/wrappers/docker_wrapper
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/unetlab/wrappers/nsenter

# --- Network plumbing: bridges, taps, namespaces, NAT ----------------------
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ip
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/brctl
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tunctl
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/ovs-vsctl
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/tc
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/iptables
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/ifconfig
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/sysctl

# --- Process and node lifecycle -------------------------------------------
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/fuser
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pkill
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/nc
# kill was removed with perl, below. Its only two call sites were the IOL
# keepalive teardown in devices/interfc.php and devices/iol/device_iol.php, both
# of which took a pid out of `ps -aux | grep`. unl_wrapper -a iol-keepalive
# --state down resolves the pids itself, from /proc, by the tenant uid that owns
# them, so no pid crosses the boundary any more.

# --- Read-only host inspection --------------------------------------------
# nproc and top were removed: counting cores and listing processes in batch
# mode are unprivileged, and the only caller (the scand_process command) was
# granted root for neither. Reading link state is unprivileged too, so
# isInterface()/isInterfaceUp() no longer sudo `ip link show` — but the ip
# grant stays, because changing links genuinely needs it.
# dmidecode was removed in Phase 05. Its callers read the machine UUID to
# fingerprint the box towards user.pnetlab.com and to show as "Box's ID";
# neither exists now. Nothing on the box needs its own hardware identity.
# ps was removed with the php line below: its one caller was
# Admin/DefaultController::upgrade(), which grepped `sudo ps -aux` for a
# running `artisan upgrade`. The worker takes a lock file now, which is what
# that grep was standing in for.
# There is deliberately no /bin/echo line. Its last caller was
# Admin/SystemController's iourc write:
#     sudo echo -e "[license]\n$license" > .../iourc
# which was injectable AND broken: sudo applied to echo while the redirect ran
# as www-data, so the write was never privileged at all — the same shape as the
# three bridge sysfs writes in includes/cli.php, recorded there. It was rewritten
# rather than relocated. refreshIolLicense() runs the keygen through proc_open()
# with an argv array and writes the file with file_put_contents(), unprivileged,
# because that is what it always was. Making it privileged would have been worse
# than the bug: the keygen lives in a tree the web user owns.

# --- Appliance control (availability impact only) -------------------------
# Both are symlinks to systemctl on a systemd host; the grant is by path, and
# systemctl behaves by argv[0], so this is not a systemctl grant -- but an
# unpinned shutdown accepts a wall message and a delay, which is a way to
# write a string to every terminal on the host as root. The two call sites
# (Admin/SystemController::shutdown() and ::reboot()) pass exactly these
# arguments and nothing else; the grants now say so.
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/shutdown -h now
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/reboot ""

# --- ROOT-EQUIVALENT. Remove these first; route behind unl_wrapper. --------
# Each of these can be turned into a root shell by anyone who controls its
# arguments. They are listed because the product does not currently work without
# them, not because granting them is acceptable.
# There is deliberately no /usr/bin/php line any more. Its last caller was
# Admin/DefaultController::upgrade(), which ran `sudo php .../artisan upgrade
# now`. The upgrade worker runs as www-data now, like the device-package
# worker: everything it does is unprivileged except one `sudo unl_wrapper -a
# package <path>`, and root decides from the signature inside that file
# whether to believe it. A php grant is a root shell for anyone who can name a
# script, and nothing in the tree needs one.
#
# There is deliberately no /usr/bin/perl line. It existed for exactly one thing:
# the IOL L1 keepalive helper, which devices/interfc.php launched as
#     sudo php .../Console/Commands/wrapper 32768 <uid> 'sudo perl .../keepalive.pl ...'
# — a script whose whole body was setgid, setuid and shell_exec of its third
# argument, i.e. "run this string as root, or first drop to any uid you name".
# It is now `unl_wrapper -a iol-keepalive -S <session> -I <iface> --state up|down`,
# which computes the uid, confirms it against the passwd entry, and execs a FIXED
# script path with an argv array. Nothing on that path builds a command line.
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/nohup             # launches anything
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/service          # launches anything
# There is deliberately no /usr/bin/docker line. All thirty-one docker
# invocations in the tree — across api.php, includes/, devices/ and store/ —
# reach the daemon over /var/run/docker.sock as a member of the docker group,
# so none of them needs sudo. SudoersPolicyTest fails on a grant the code no
# longer invokes, so re-adding this line breaks the build until a call site
# needs it again.
#
# Group membership is not a smaller privilege: anyone who can talk to the
# daemon can bind-mount / into a container. It is one path instead of two, and
# it replaced an unauthenticated daemon socket on 127.0.0.1:4243, which any
# local account could reach with no membership at all.
# idlepc is GONE, and it was the worst line in this file. The grant ran a 9.4 MB
# stripped PyInstaller bundle committed to this repository with no source, no
# build recipe and no licence — Python 3.5 with paramiko, cryptography, PyNaCl
# and OpenSSL frozen inside it. The archive was unpacked and read, and what it
# did is worse than what it was. idlepc.py, in order:
#
#   1. ssh-keygen -t rsa -N '' -f /root/.ssh/id_rsa_dy      (no passphrase)
#   2. cat /root/.ssh/id_rsa_dy.pub >> /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
#   3. paramiko connect to root@127.0.0.1 with that key
#   4. run dynamips in that session, send  ('i') to trigger dynamips'
#      OWN idle-pc computation, and scrape "--idle-pc=([^"]+)" from the console
#   5. pkill -9 -f "dynamips ..."
#
# So pressing "calculate Idle-PC" in the admin UI PERMANENTLY INSTALLED A
# PASSWORDLESS ROOT SSH KEY on the appliance — the same standing root key this
# fork had already deleted from docker_wrapper by allocating a PTY instead of
# shelling out to root@localhost, reintroduced by an admin button.
#
# None of it was doing anything dynamips cannot. Step 4 is dynamips' built-in
# calculation, and the SSH session existed only to obtain a TTY, because the
# blob ran dynamips with no -T and dynamips with no -T puts its console on
# stdin. dynamips 0.2.14's common/dev_vtty.c routes the TCP console created by
# `-T <port>` through the SAME input state machine, Ctrl-] included, so the
# replacement drives it over a plain socket and needs no terminal at all:
# `unl_wrapper -a idlepc --template <name> --image <name>`, implemented in
# platform/wrappers/actions/UnlIdlePc.php. The caller sends two names; the
# option string, the paths, the console port and the process lifetime all live
# on this side of the boundary.
#
# The calibration itself is UNPROVEN, because it needs a Cisco IOS image and
# this project carries none. The action says so in its header and
# docs/LICENSING.md section 3 says so at length. Removing the root key was not
# contingent on proving the replacement.
www-data ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/rm                # arbitrary deletion
# tee and touch are gone, and they went together. Both had exactly one caller,
# Query::setProxy(), which was the shortest path from an HTTP request to root in
# this tree:
#     if(!is_file($file)) exec('sudo touch '.$file);
#     $result = exec("echo '".$proxyAddr."' | sudo tee ".$file);
# with $proxyAddr concatenated from four request fields inside a SINGLE-QUOTED
# shell string — one apostrophe ends the quoting — and the destination an apt
# configuration file, which can carry APT::Update::Pre-Invoke and therefore run
# as root at the next apt run. Two root primitives from one admin form. It is now
# `unl_wrapper -a set-proxy`, which takes the four components as four validated
# options, takes the password on stdin so it is not in /proc/<pid>/cmdline, and
# writes a file whose path is a constant in the action.
#
# chown is gone. Its six call sites were four constants in
# User/DependenceController, one in User/VersionsController, and
#     exec('sudo chown www-data:www-data '. $file);
# in Admin/DefaultController, where $file came from $req->input('template')
# through secureCmd() — a blocklist that passes backticks, $( ) and quotes. All
# six are `unl_wrapper -a fixperms --scope <word>`: the caller sends one of six
# scope words, never a path, and an unrecognised scope is refused rather than
# interpreted.
#
# chmod is gone with them. Its last caller chmodded the IOL keygen, which sits in
# /opt/unetlab/addons — a tree the fixperms 'addons' scope hands to www-data, so
# the web user can chmod its own file without borrowing root to do it.
# cp, mv, mkdir and link are gone. Every one of them was reached from the QEMU
# commit flow in Admin/Node_sessionsController.php, on paths concatenated from
# request input without a quote anywhere:
#     $newFolder = '/opt/unetlab/addons/qemu/' . $imageQemu[0] . '-' . $deviceName;
#     exec('sudo mkdir ' . $newFolder);
#     exec('sudo cp -f ' . $qcowFile . ' ' . $newFolder . '/' . basename($qcowFile));
# That flow is now `unl_wrapper -a image-commit`, which receives a session id, a
# type word from a closed set and a slug — never a path. link had no live call
# site at all: devices/qemu/device_qemu.php assigned the command to $cmd and
# never executed it.
# qemu-img is GONE. It had one caller left, the dependency listing in
# Admin/LabsController::getDepends(), and the note that used to stand here
# predicted this: `qemu-img info --backing-chain` is a read that needs no
# privilege at all. Measured on the reference host — as www-data, against
# /opt/unetlab/addons/qemu — it returns the full chain with exit 0 and no sudo.
#
# The call site was also the worst-shaped one left in the tree: it interpolated
# an unescaped path into a shell string, and that path came from the `image`
# attribute of an uploaded lab's XML. It is now proc_open() with an argv array,
# so there is no shell and no sudo. The grant could read or WRITE any file on
# the host as root, which is more than a listing ever needed.
# useradd is gone. It had exactly one caller, checkUsername() in
# includes/cli.php:
#     sudo /usr/sbin/useradd -c "Unified Networking Lab TID=N" -d /opt/unetlab/users/N
#          -g unl -M -s /bin/bash -u <32768+N> unlN
# and that sudo was never buying anything: checkUsername() is reached only from a
# device's prepare(), which is reached only from `unl_wrapper -a start`, which is
# already root. It was root running sudo to become root, and the cost of it was a
# standing grant on a binary that can create a second uid 0 account.
#
# Account creation is now UnlTenantAccount::create(), which execs useradd with an
# argv array from inside the wrapper. Removal — which did not exist at all, in
# any form, anywhere in the tree — is `unl_wrapper -a reap-tenant -S <session>`
# or `--scope all`. The caller sends a session id or the word 'all' and never a
# name; the account must be unl<digits>, must hold exactly uid 32768+session, and
# must have `unl` as its primary group.

# --- Deliberately ABSENT. Do not reinstate. --------------------------------
#   www-data ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL
#   %www-data ALL=(ALL:ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL
#   %unl     ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL
